    "summary": "Somaliland's administration responds to Somali President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud's Al Jazeera interview, asserting its right to pursue diplomatic ties and a sea access deal with Ethiopia.",
    "content": "The Somaliland administration has issued a statement in response to remarks made by Somali President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud during an interview with Al Jazeera. The interview touched on Somaliland's relations with Israel, the port city of Berbera, and the 2024 memorandum of understanding between Ethiopia and Somaliland. In its statement, Somaliland's Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation emphasized that Somalia does not have the authority to determine Somaliland's diplomatic relations or to speak on behalf of its people.\n\nThe 2024 agreement between Ethiopia and Somaliland, which would grant Ethiopia access to the sea, has been a point of contention. Somaliland has consistently defended its right to enter into such agreements, while Somalia has opposed the deal, viewing it as a violation of its sovereignty. The recent exchange highlights the ongoing tension between the two sides over Somaliland's international engagements.\n\nSomaliland's foreign ministry reiterated its position that it is prepared to move forward with the sea access agreement with Ethiopia. The statement did not provide new details about the implementation of the deal but reaffirmed Somaliland's willingness to cooperate on the matter. This comes amid broader regional discussions about trade routes and maritime access in the Horn of Africa.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is significant as it underscores the deep political divisions within Somali territories. Many in the diaspora have ties to both Somalia and Somaliland, and the dispute over Somaliland's diplomatic autonomy and its agreements with foreign nations can affect family, business, and political affiliations. The diaspora often plays a role in mediating or advocating for dialogue, and this latest exchange may reignite debates within diaspora communities about recognition and self-determination.\n\nAs the situation evolves, stakeholders in the region and beyond will be watching closely. The Somali federal government has not yet issued a response to Somaliland's latest statement. The international community, including the African Union and neighboring countries, continues to monitor the dispute, which has implications for stability and cooperation in the Horn of Africa.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/04/maamulka-soomaaliland-oo-sheegay-inuu-diyaar-u-yahay-heshiis-itoobiya-bad-lagu-siinayo/",
